  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point toward temperature-controlled chemical ordering as a strong determinant of plasticity. R2 and R14 connect FCC local or long-range order and increased antiphase-boundary energy with altered dislocation motion; R8 shows that strong ordering can cause brittleness and that weakened order can restore ductility; and R18 identifies pronounced ordering or phase-separation changes near 600°C in a closely related Al–Co–Cr–Fe–Ni system. R3 and R6 also establish thermal processing of AlCoCrFeNi2.1, with R6 explicitly identifying L12 and B2 constituents. Nevertheless, none reports intermediate-temperature aging of as-cast AlCoCrFeNi2.1 producing the specific 600°C ductility minimum and 750°C recovery, nor do the records identify jagged B2/FCC boundaries, boundary-tip stress concentration, or their coupling to L12-induced planar slip. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but reconstructing the specific non-monotonic transition and coupled mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
}
